abstract="This chapter reviews models for gas markets that serve as a mathematical abstraction of the entry-exit system employed in the European gas market. A particular focus is on a four-level model where, in the first level, the transmission system operator sets technical capacities and (lower bounds on) booking prices for network nodes. In the second level, producers and consumers book capacities at their respective entry and exit points covering multiple time periods. In the third level, producers and consumers nominate production and consumption for the individual periods, and in the fourth level, the transmission system operator realizes the gas flow in all time periods based on the nominated gas demands of producers and consumers. Further modeling variants are discussed.",
